Yes—you can both support any number of init webservers automatically and reserve dedicated CPU/memory capacity on them so web traffic spikes never starve NAT gateway packet forwarding.
By splitting the setup by hostname (*init* vs. autoscaled) and isolating the web stack inside a resource-capped systemd slice, your init webservers will always keep guaranteed headroom for Linux kernel packet forwarding (iptables / conntrack) even if PHP or Nginx gets hammered.

How the Complete Architecture Works

1. init Webservers (1 to N servers):
	• Enable Linux IP forwarding + iptables MASQUERADE so they act as NAT gateways.
	• Cap Nginx + PHP-FPM inside a systemd slice (e.g., 75% CPU and 75% RAM max), leaving 25% of the machine permanently reserved for the kernel NAT router and SSH.
	• Publish their private VPC IPs to a shared list in your S3 datastore (or ADT runtime directory) so autoscaled webservers discover all init gateways automatically—no hardcoded IPs needed.
2. Autoscaled Webservers (non-init):
	• Read the dynamic list of init private VPC IPs.
	• Run a lightweight failover daemon that checks all init gateways in order, routes DBaaS traffic through the first healthy init server, and seamlessly rotates to the next init server in 3 seconds if one goes down or is destroyed.

Step 1: On Every Webserver Build (init vs. Autoscaled)

Add this logic to your ADT webserver post-build hook (e.g., in adt-webserver-scripts):
bash
#!/bin/bash
set -e

HOSTNAME_NOW=$(hostname)
PUBLIC_IF="eth0"          # Adjust if your provider uses ens3/enp1s0
PRIVATE_IF="eth1"         # Your private VPC interface
VPC_CIDR="10.0.0.0/16"    # Your regional VPC CIDR

if [[ "${HOSTNAME_NOW}" == *init* ]]; then
    # ==========================================================
    # ROLE A: INIT WEBSERVER (Acts as NAT Gateway + Capped Web)
    # ==========================================================

    # 1. Enable IPv4 forwarding and NAT masquerading
    echo "net.ipv4.ip_forward=1" > /etc/sysctl.d/99-nat-gateway.conf
    sysctl -p /etc/sysctl.d/99-nat-gateway.conf

    iptables -t nat -F POSTROUTING
    iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -s "${VPC_CIDR}" -o "${PUBLIC_IF}" -j MASQUERADE

    DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get update -qq
    DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y -qq iptables-persistent
    netfilter-persistent save

    # 2. Reserve 25% CPU & RAM for NAT forwarding (cap web stack at 75%)
    cat << 'EOF' > /etc/systemd/system/webstack.slice
[Unit]
Description=Resource-capped slice for Nginx and PHP-FPM on init servers

[Slice]
CPUQuota=75%
MemoryMax=75%
MemoryHigh=70%
EOF

    # Assign Nginx and PHP-FPM services into the capped slice
    for SVC in nginx php8.1-fpm php8.2-fpm php8.3-fpm; do
        if systemctl list-unit-files | grep -q "^${SVC}.service"; then
            mkdir -p "/etc/systemd/system/${SVC}.service.d"
            cat << 'EOF' > "/etc/systemd/system/${SVC}.service.d/slice.conf"
[Service]
Slice=webstack.slice
EOF
        fi
    done

    systemctl daemon-reload
    systemctl restart nginx 2>/dev/null || true
    systemctl restart php*-fpm 2>/dev/null || true

    # 3. Record this init server's private VPC IP (upload to S3 datastore via s3cmd/awscli)
    MY_PRIVATE_IP=$(ip -4 addr show "${PRIVATE_IF}" | awk '/inet / {print $2}' | cut -d/ -f1 | head -n1)
    echo "${MY_PRIVATE_IP}" > "/tmp/init-gw-${HOSTNAME_NOW}.ip"
    # Example ADT datastore push (adapt to your s3cmd/datastore helper):
    # /usr/bin/s3cmd put "/tmp/init-gw-${HOSTNAME_NOW}.ip" "s3://${S3_BUCKET}/gateways/${REGION}/"
fi
Use code with caution.

How the Capacity Reservation Protects the Gateway

• CPUQuota=75%: Even if PHP-FPM spawns max workers during a traffic spike, Nginx + PHP combined can never consume more than 75% of total CPU cores—leaving 25% dedicated to kernel interrupt handling (ksoftirqd) and packet NAT translation.
• MemoryMax=75%: Prevents runaway PHP scripts from exhausting system RAM and triggering the Linux OOM-killer or starving nf_conntrack network tables.
• Kernel Priority: Because iptables MASQUERADE runs inside the Linux kernel network stack rather than user space, reserving that 25% CPU/RAM headroom guarantees near-zero packet forwarding latency even at 100% web stack saturation.

Step 2: Dynamic Multi-Gateway Failover on Autoscaled Webservers


You populate /etc/dbaas_init_gateways.list with the private VPC IPv4 addresses of your init webservers (one per line), and you leave /run/dbaas_active_routes completely empty—the script manages it automatically.
So, the put the private ip addresses of the init webservers into this file which can be discovered by the autoscalers and passed to the autoscaled
webservers as part of their cloud-init process

1. GATEWAY_LIST_FILE="/etc/dbaas_init_gateways.list"

This is the input file that tells each autoscaled webserver which init servers it can use as NAT gateways.
• Format: Plain text, one private VPC IP address per line, with no spaces, quotes, or ports.
• Example contents (if you built three init webservers in that region):text
10.10.0.12
10.10.0.13
10.10.0.14
Use code with caution.
• Important: Use the init webservers' private VPC IPs (e.g., 10.x.x.x or 192.168.x.x on eth1), not their public IPs—otherwise ip route replace ... via <GW_IP> will fail with Nexthop has invalid gateway.
• How it gets populated in ADT: During your initial build, each init webserver writes its private VPC IP to your S3 datastore bucket (or ADT build machine), and each newly autoscaled webserver downloads and combines those IPs into /etc/dbaas_init_gateways.list on boot.

2. STATE_FILE="/run/dbaas_active_routes"

Do not put anything in this file manually.
• What it is: An internal scratchpad stored in RAM (/run) that the failover script writes to itself every 3 seconds.
• What the script writes inside it: Pairs of <resolved_dbaas_ip>:<active_gateway_ip> (for example, 52.94.12.88:10.10.0.12).
• Why it exists: If your DBaaS uses a dynamic DNS hostname (like AWS Aurora) and its underlying IP address rotates during a database failover, the script compares the new DNS IP against /run/dbaas_active_routes so it knows which old /32 route to delete from the Linux routing table.
